From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hunter B Hough <git@hough.ink>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 01:26:48 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 165/179] fix(gastown): drop the dead session-bead liveness leg
 and score a zero-row roster as unconfirmed (gcp-dfmw)

recover-orphaned-beads built its liveness map from two reads. The second,
`gc bd list --type=session --label=gc:session ...`, returns zero rows in every
context: session beads resolve by `gc bd show <id>` but cannot be enumerated by
`gc bd list` from the rig or HQ ledger, and a real session bead carries no
configured_named_identity, so even a working list would resolve nothing. The
leg contributed nothing, and a later change that surfaces stderr from that
redirect would hand jq non-JSON, zero the map and disable recovery city-wide.

Separately, the guard scored a blind read as CONFIRMED absent: it returned
unusable only when MAP_COUNT==0 with SESSION_COUNT>0, so a roster read that
succeeds with `{"sessions":[]}` built an empty map and every assignee read as
absent. The witness running this recipe is itself a session, so a zero-row
roster is a blind read, not an empty city.

This removes the session-bead query, its temp file, the jq reduce and the
prose that described two legs (formula and witness prompt), states that the
roster is the only liveness source, and makes build_liveness_map return 1 on
SESSION_COUNT==0. The FAIL-SAFE block gets a "zero rows" arm, guarded on a
successful query so a failed read still reports "could not be built". The
"FAIL-SAFE: empty liveness map" text is kept: the inference gate pins it.

The session-bead test fixture goes with the leg, and
test_zero_row_source_is_unconfirmed_not_absent covers the new rule. It fails
on the unchanged formula ("expected STILL_ORPHANED=false, got 'true'") and
fails again with the SESSION_COUNT guard deleted.

Not changed: the polecat_session / null-assignee fallback (superseded by
gcp-952j9), gcp-4k6o, Step 2a, Step 3, 3a and 3b.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
